= Defining play

== Background

Hans defines play as follows:

1. "The essence of play is its capacity to saturate virtually every aspect of our lives, though not continuously." #cite(<hansPlayWorld1981>, 2)
2. "The willingness to forego one's own territory, to be willing to pass beyond what one knows one is capable of, is the fundamental feature of play." #cite(<hansPlayWorld1981>, 13) 
3. "Still, whereas play is free in the sense that there is no Being in which to ground it, it is also bound in two crucial senses: first the beginning of play is always necessarily connected to a foreproject, to a series of prejudgments that are at issue in the activity of play itself, that give an orientation for the play; and second, the result of play is a structure, a framework or order that has been confirmed by the play itself." #cite(<hansPlayWorld1981>, 10)

He is quite clearly talking about _transformative play_ as defined by
#cite(<salentekinbasRulesPlayGame2004>, 305). "Transformative play is a special
case of play that occurs when the free movement of play alters the more rigid
structure in which it takes shape. The play [...] transforms the space as a
whole." In fact, they quote Hans here: "The role of play is not to work
comfortably within its own structures but rather constantly to develop its
structures through play." #cite(<hansPlayWorld1981>, 5)

Also though, play in this sense is entangled, as in meta-gaming: transformative
play is bound by rules but at the same time changes them.

I would say that transformative play _must first be bound by rules,_ what Hans
would call the 'foreproject.' If there are no rules there is nothing to play
against. It is only with restriction that we can play at all. See def. of play
in _Rules of Play_  as "free movement within a more rigid structure"
#cite(<salentekinbasRulesPlayGame2004>, 304). Hans talks about play in both the
his coined transformative sense, but also in the broader sense as defined by
Salen and Zimmerman. When Hans shifts from an agential structure (focusing on
activity, especially human activity) to discuss the playful structure of nature
he describes play as "limited and not clearly definable -- some of our 'laws' of
physics, for example, point to the present limits of natural play -- but within
those limits natural play is capable of an infinite number of new grafts and
connections." #cite(<hansPlayWorld1981>, "16-17") #footnote[This sense of
natural play is somewhat reminiscent of Huizinga's comsology of play.]

I read Hans as having several admixed conceptions of play. The first is
agential, having to do with action, the second is onto-epistemological, having
to do with our understanding of the world, and the third is mechanical (what
Hans terms "natural"), having to do with the free play of entities within a
structure. I will call the first _agentic play,_ the second _theoretic play,_
and the third _mechanical play._ Agentic play, claims Hans, is the basic
attitude of our interaction with the world. This feeds into our theoretic play,
which has to do with the our conceptual understanding and modelling of the
world. The reason this all works out so well is that the physical world is
itself in a state of "natural play," exploring the freedom within the natural
laws. In the end, all play is supposed to be based on natural play, or play in
the broadest sense. Mechanical play is the sort of play that occurs between the
fittings of a copper pipe, or the turn of a screwdriver. Mechanical play, and
thus play most broadly, is free movement within the constraints of set of rules.

Let's take a second here to clarify the notion of mechanical play in its
relation to nature. First off, when I speak of the laws of physics, I should not
be construed to be speaking about physical phenomena which are essential,
structuring aspects of the universe, but rather statistical propositions which
require certain magnitudes of scale. Certain macro-level laws do not apply
at the micro scale. For example the laws of thermodynamics are, in Deleuze and
Guattari's
phrasing#footnote[#_cite(<deleuzeAntiOedipusCapitalismSchizophrenia2009>,
supplement: [_passim_])], _molar_ having to do with emergent properties of large
masses of objects, while particle laws are _molecular,_ having to do with much
smaller collections, down to the individual. In this sense then the rules of
physics are "loose," although mathematically concrete. Put another way, physical
laws are not and cannot be totalizing, because no mathematical structure could
capture laws for both micro and macro level phenomena at once. While we
symbolize these phenomena through the language of empirical-statistical
mathematics, we do not thereby capture the real, total behavior of our object of
study.

Mechanical play happens in the boundaries between these laws, in the space in
which behavior cannot be pinned down. As a metaphor, consider the movement of
beer in a closed up bottle. As the container shifts around, dynamic whorls and
pools of liquid form, bubbles foam and pop. But at any given time the liquid
remains within the container, constrained by its own particular medium and the
resistance of the glass. It is in fact the container which gives it shape. And
no amount of wiggling will change the properties of the liquid. This is why I
consider this sort of play _mechanical._ We will later consider transformative
play, which, extending our physical metaphor, could be considered chemical. 

Hans, however, takes this notion of mechanical play and forms it as the basis of
his argument. Throughout the book, this single notion of play is at play. It is
hard to square this with his conception of play as mimesis. Surely for humans
this is at least plausible -- a copy with difference at the heart of the social
play which then becomes transformative through that very difference -- but at a
physical-mechanical level it is hard to justify. Personifying physical matter,
Hans might be compelled to say that particles themselves are imitating one
another, though imperfectly, and that it is this imperfect copying which gives
rise to playful activity. But this is not the sort of play which happens on an
atomic level: there is no replication-with-difference. If we are able to place
this idea onto any scientific field it might well be evolutionary biology.
Repetition-with-difference is at the very heart of the biological process; it is
what makes evolution what it is. But at this point the biological and physical
playstyles must be made distinct. For physical playstyles we retain the phrase
mechanical play, while for biological playstyles we will use the phrase
_transformative play._

At this point one has to wonder whether this account of play will hinge on a
deterministic or nondeterministic reading of quantum mechanics. Such a hinge may
well have a large deductive outcome for our argument, especially if we are to
follow through on our reading of mechanical play, as it is clear that biological
phenomena (the realm of transformative play) will necessarily supervene on
physical phenomena (the realm of mechanical play). To acquiesce to determinism
will lead us down the road of chaos theory. To allow for random chance will give
us a base-level statistical analysis of the phenomena. Yet in either case, the
outcome is the same: there is _apparent_ randomness in the behavior of
thermodynamic particles; there is _apparent_ chaos in the movement of fluids.
Whatever the underlying mechanics of the universe are -- whether set by
immutable, hard laws, or statistical laws of ever-diminishing proportions -- the
observable outcome is the same. Our goal is to discuss the _epistemological_
role of play, so, for our purposes, that is enough.

Hans' understanding of theoretic play is based on the Husserlian notion of
horizon. He refutes Derrida's collapse of the ontological into the
epistemological, offering instead a nuanced, layered approach. #quote(block:true, attribution:cite(<hansPlayWorld1981>, 21), [\[T\]he
epistemological is always that which we understand of the ontological, so at any
given moment it may appear as though the ontological and the epistemological are
the same. But in fact their horizons are never quite the same, for the
epistemological is also always structured, at least implicitly, in terms of that
which it does not encompass, in terms of that which does not make 'sense'
according to its own understanding. And that which does not make sense or has
not yet been understood points to the larger horizon of the ontological.])

So, our epistemological understandings are
limited at their horizon by the ontological. By way of example, consider a
formal mathematical system. No sufficiently complex formal system can be
complete, in the sense offered by Gödel's theorems. They have holes where no
proof can provide an answer. However, that is not to say that the there is no
truth of the matter: true but unprovable statements exist, as do undecidable
questions. We often turn to a different formal system -- a metalanguage -- in
order to move past the limitations of the base-level language. But this repeats
ad infinitum. At the limit of the recursion we broach what Hans would term the
ontological: the purely conceptual stratum of ideal objects which no single
formal system (epistemology) can broach.

There is no single ontology, no single epistemology. How to choose to orient
ourselves, that is, which epistemology we take up, in part determines the objects of our world.

#quote(attribution: cite(<hansPlayWorld1981>, "21-22"), block: true, [To begin to think of man as a purely physical,
psycho-chemical creature provides new possibilities for ontological grafts, just
as thinking of man as a purely linguistic creature would. And any basic change
in our conception of social structure could easily enough lead to
redistributions of populations, resources, and systems in ways which could
profoundly affect the ontological system.]) 

The objects of our ontology vary based on the epistemology we presuppose. How we
choose to frame our view of the world -- the rules of the game, the hues of our attitudes --
effects what objects we can discern. 

But the objects within our world themselves circle down to affect our
epistemology. "Play itself is the field within which the ontological framework
comes to be better understood; and it is that better understanding [...] that
makes grafts onto the ontological possible." #cite(<hansPlayWorld1981>, 22) Play
makes a change in epistemological standpoint possible, which itself allows the
ontology to change, which loops back down and feeds into the epistemological.
Play, Hans says, is dialectical. He argues that the changes which play can
affect are gradual, though his reasoning (AT LEAST IN CHAPTER 1) is unclear on
this point. #footnote[TODO: revise this section https://pi.dev/session/#c462993f97a7b922d30c76dd154a7638 -- later chapter on the aesthetic and Einstein makes this clear]

Noë claims the aesthetic attitude is _fragile._ He lists two components of
aesthetic fragility which I will term _non-compellingness_ and _incompleteness._
I focus here on incompleteness. As in the mathematical examples given above, no
aesthetic experience is total. When viewing a mug, for example, we see its
front, and know on its internal horizon that it has a back and sides. Any
articulation of an aesthetic object must then be incomplete. "When we talk, for
example, we always run the risk of being misunderstood, and so we need to stand
ready, always, to rephrase, reformulate, rearticulate." And this fragility is an
essential aspect of the structure of our aesthetic phenomena in general. "A
language that admitted no communication breakdown within language would be like
a visual perception in which everything was visible from all points of view at
once. These would be language or perception missing the essential aspect of
fragility, and these would be unrecognizable as language or perception in the
sense that we know" #cite(<noeEntanglementHowArt2023a>, 174).

Simultaneously, for the very reason of its non-totality, aesthetic experiences
are non-compelling, caught between the subjective and objective standpoints. Our
aesthetic experiences are situated on the horizon _between_ the objective and
subjective. It is so to speak a step behind, a bracketing of the particular
structures in which we so naturally find ourselves engaged, a birds-eye view of
the structure of our rationality. As Hans puts it, object and subject emerge out
of the aesthetic attitude as the epistemological subject and its corresponding
ontological object. The aesthetic attitude itself underpins this very split: it
is a phenomenological standpoint by which we first come to terms with the rules
of play which define for us what objects there are and what methods we have for
learning about them.


---

C. Thi Nguyen argues in _Games: Agency as Art_ that games are the artform of
agency. His conception of play is similar to Hans' conception of agentic play.
Nguyen's project is to defend a notion of aesthetic striving play. Striving play
is a form of _game_ play, i.e., play defined by rule-bound restrictions, in
which one fully immerses oneself in the ends of the game, often for a
higher-level purpose. For example, one may play a game of chess to teach their
daughter, or a round of _Everything is Crab_ to get some whimsy after a
stressful day at work. Yet we do not pursue the ends of the game for their own
sake: they are what Nguyen calls "disposable ends." These are ends which we take
up with full force as an attempt to reach some extrinsic goal, whether that be
relaxation, education, exercise, or an attempt at experiencing beauty. It is
this last form of striving play that Nguyen focuses on and terms _aesthetic
striving play._

While I appreciate Nguyen's tight focus on game play, in this essay I want to broaden the horizons of what play can do. 

